# Worked example

A single feature walked through the seven phases, so the *shape* of each artifact is visible.
Replace it with one of your own once you have one — a real example from this repository teaches
more than an invented one.

## 0. Track — the epic

One line is correct at creation. Its only job is to record that a problem exists.

```markdown
# Epic: uploads time out on large files

## Problem
Files over ~50 MB fail with a gateway timeout. Users retry, which makes it worse.
```

Fileable without knowing the solution. That is the test.

## 1. Spec — only if there are real open questions

Objective, boundaries, success criteria, open questions. A spec earns its existence by surfacing
something you do not yet know; if its Open Questions section is empty, the plan absorbs it.

## 2. Plan — ordered, verifiable steps

Header names the goal, the architecture, and the **PR boundaries** — one line per pull request,
each naming the child issue it closes. Then bite-sized tasks: write the failing test, run it, make
it pass, run it again, commit.

The plan gets a staff-engineer review before any code is written. Mechanical findings are applied
and listed; judgment findings are surfaced and block.

## 3-4. Build and verify

Thin slices, each leaving the tree green. `./verify.sh` in each component you touched, before
pushing — the same script CI runs.

## 5-6. Review and merge

Code review and security review against the pending diff. Then CI is the gate: the required
checks decide, not a human's reading of the diff.

```markdown
## Children
- [x] #41 — stream uploads to storage instead of buffering
- [ ] #42 — surface progress in the client
```

## 7. Document

An ADR when a choice would be expensive to reverse. Say what was rejected and why — the
alternatives are the part that is hard to reconstruct later.
